==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From the {{inh|en|enm|ransoun}}, from the {{der|en|fro|raençon}}, from stem of {{der|en|la|redemptio}}. Entered English ca. the 13th century. {{doublet|en|redemption}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɹænsəm/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-ransom.wav |Audio (Southern England)}}
* {{hyph|en|ran|som}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-|s}}

# [[money|Money]] paid for the [[free]]ing of a [[hostage]].
#: {{ux|en|They were held for two million dollars '''ransom'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|They were held to '''ransom'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=12|passage=Thy '''ransom''' paid, which man from death redeems.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1612|author={{w|John Davies (poet)|John Davies}}|title=Discoverie of the True Causes why Ireland was never entirely subdued
|passage=His captivity in Austria, and the heavy '''ransom''' he paid for his liberty.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2010|author=Caroline Alexander|title=The War That Killed Achilles: The True Story of Homer's Iliad
|passage=As rich as was the '''ransom''' Priam paid for Hektor, Hermes says, his remaining sons at Troy “'would give three times as much ransom / for you, who are alive, were Atreus' son Agamemnon / to recognize you.'”}}
# The release of a captive, or of captured property, by payment of a consideration.
#: {{ux|en|prisoners hopeless of '''ransom'''}}
#* {{RQ:Dryden Iliad|Ilias 1|passage=Till the fair slave be rendered to her sire; And '''ransom'''-free restored to his abode}}
# {{lb|en|historical|legal|UK}} A [[sum]] paid for the [[pardon]] of some great offence and the [[discharge]] of the offender; also, a fine paid in lieu of corporal punishment.
#* {{RQ:Blackstone Commentaries|passage=punished with imprisonment and '''ransom''' at the King's will}}

====Usage notes====
* "held {{m|en|for}} ransom" is much more common in the US, "held {{m|en|to}} ransom" in the UK.

